Key facts established:

  - CI: tests.yml runs on push/PR on 4 platforms WITHOUT OpenMS, only builds SPEXTRACTOR_TESTS_ONLY (calibration header test). build.yml (weekly) is the only job that compiles the tool — and it runs weekly, not per push. So the v2.0.0 tool itself is NOT built by CI per push; "the only job that proves the tool compiles" runs weekly. CONTRIBUTING says build.yml "is the reference recipe" ✓. But: v1.1.0 changelog says "CI runs on four platforms per push (Linux GCC 13 and 11, macOS arm64, Windows MSVC)". tests.yml: ubuntu-latest gcc (version? not pinned — ubuntu-latest currently gcc 13/14, ok), ubuntu-22.04 gcc-11, macos appleclang, windows msvc. But these only build the calibration header tests, not the tool. v1.1.0's phrasing "CI runs on four platforms per push" is arguably misleading but was v1.1.0. More importantly: README Requirements says "C++20 — GCC 13+ or Clang 16+" — but CI builds (tests-only) with gcc-11, and macOS appleclang (AppleClang version not 16+ Clang equivalent necessarily). Also the full tool build (build.yml) uses ubuntu-latest default gcc. GCC 11 cannot compile the tool if it truly needs C++20 features beyond gcc11 — but tests-only target compiles with gcc-11, meaning the claim "GCC 13+" is for the tool; CI doesn't test the tool with gcc-11. Consistent-ish. However README says "Clang 16+" — macos-latest appleclang is not Clang 16 exactly; and tests-only compiles under it. Minor.

  - Wait, important: v1.1.0 changelog says "CI runs on four platforms per push (Linux GCC 13 and 11, macOS arm64, Windows MSVC) and a weekly job builds the tool against an OpenMS source tree patched by scripts/apply_openms_patches.sh". The four-platform job does NOT build the tool (only tests-only targets). The sentence implies CI per push builds... ambiguous. But the bigger release-engineering defect: at v2.0.0, with a mandatory static_assert on the third patch, NO per-push CI compiles the tool at all. The weekly build.yml is the only compile check. Also: does tests.yml even compile src/spextractor.cpp? No. So a release cut could ship a tree that doesn't compile until next Monday's cron. That's a release-engineering defect worth flagging (HIGH/MEDIUM). Did they run build.yml before release? Unknown.

  - Test file: checks c1..c6, then `cg(label=label...)` — check 7/8 via loop (gap cycles?), c9..c13. So 13 checks total presumably. "check(name, fn)" counts. Need to see how many check() calls: c1..c6 = 6, cg loop — how many iterations? Lines 278-310: `def c6():` then a loop with `def cg(...)` and presumably two calls (labels) → checks 7 and 8. Then c9..c13 = 5. Total 13 ✓. CHANGELOG says "the suite ran every check on the openms fallback before 9 and 10 existed" — need to verify: checks c9/c10 are about the shipped detector. Claim: previously every check ran on openms fallback. Check the test file near run() / detector_of and how detector is chosen. Let me read lines 216-379 of the test to verify the 13 checks and what they do, plus the threads determinism check (1 vs 4 threads).

