  Q1 answer: Yes. Rename (breaking CLI), mandatory third patch (breaking build), changed defaults (breaking behavior/output). Semver major is not just defensible but required by the rename alone. Caveat: output-not-digest-comparable by itself wouldn't force major (bug fixes change output), but it's moot. Also process note: two majors' worth of churn 4 days after 1.0.0 suggests 1.x was cut prematurely, but that's not a semver violation. Also note the "private and public repository share one version line" — the jump from private v38 to public 2.0.0 is a mapping decision, fine.

  Q2 answer: Implementation is correct as far as it goes: both version_ and verboseVersion_ are set (verboseVersion_ is what --help prints; line 1660-1661), CMake injects PROJECT_VERSION, CITATION.cff matches. Risks: (a) ini round-trip: inis written by 1.x carry the OpenMS version string; TOPPBase will warn on version mismatch — harmless and arguably desirable for a breaking release; (b) the mzML software entry and spx userParams now carry 2.0.0 — any downstream parser expecting an OpenMS version there breaks, but that's the intent; (c) official=false is correctly set (ToolHandler) — the comment documents the old name had been registered as an official TOPP tool; (d) residual: two sources of version truth (hardcoded fallback define vs CMake) can drift; (e) OpenMS-internal version parsing ("x.y.z" format) — "2.0.0" parses cleanly. No correctness bug; the compatibility effects are the intended break and are disclosed in "Why 2.0".

  Q3 answer: the number inconsistencies (list them compactly): 178k/93k vs 117k/73k; 36.3/8.0x unsupported; 138 GB vs ~99 GB; 2.8-2.9 vs 2.91-2.94 vs own 2.9; ~13% vs 11.3% vs README 11-26%; 112-138→7.8 vs 23.4x (16.5x direct); 57 s vs 0:42; 4,035,058 vs 4,035,234; 5,313→5,322 not in record; 80-125 GB stale.

